6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2} 
Building directly on the liberty-count details from Col3/Col4/Col6/Col7, B2 is the single forcing move: the connected A2-B3-B4-C1-C2 cluster has exactly the three liberties A3/A4/B2, and B2 is the only legal play that drops it to two while simultaneously putting both the isolated A2 stone and the C1-C2 pair into atari. Any Black reply at A3, A4 or B1 lets White answer on the final shared liberty and complete the capture before an eye can form at A1. 

By contrast, the A4/B1 suggestions from Col1 and Col5 leave Black an immediate tempo to occupy B2 himself, restoring a liberty and creating an escape route or false eye; those points only threaten isolated stones rather than the whole group. Distant candidates such as D3 or C5 are too slow. The apparent “illegal/self-atari” objection to B2 does not hold—B1 remains empty and adjacent, so the move is legal and keeps the net closed. Thus B2 is the unique first move that guarantees capture regardless of Black’s replies.

4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2} 
Building directly on the liberty counts and forcing sequences laid out by colleagues 2, 3, 4, 6 and 7: the connected A2-B3-B4-C1-C2 cluster really does have exactly three liberties at A3/A4/B2. B2 is the single legal point that simultaneously drops that group to two liberties, places both the A2 and C1-C2 subgroups in atari, and leaves White the follow-up tempo to answer any Black reply (A3, A4 or B1) by filling the last liberty before Black can form an eye or connect. 

B1, by contrast, only ataris the C1-C2 stones while leaving the B3-B4 group untouched; Black can simply answer at B2 (or even A3), regaining a liberty and breaking the net. A4 is likewise only a one-sided atari that permits Black to play B2 himself and create breathing room. Claims that A4 or B1 work therefore overlook the shared-liberty structure and the tempo Black gains. All other candidates remain either suicidal or too distant to reduce the critical liberties in time. Thus B2 remains the unique move that initiates a forced capture against every possible Black reply.

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2} 
B2 remains the unique forcing first move. It fills the critical shared liberty among the connected Black stones (A2-B3-B4-C1-C2), reducing them to exactly two liberties at A3/A4 while simultaneously putting A2 and the C1-C2 stones in atari. Any Black reply at A3, A4 or B1 can be met by White answering at the last remaining liberty on the subsequent turn, completing the capture before Black can form two eyes or connect outward. 

B1 fails as a starter because it only ataris the isolated C1-C2 stones; Black can simply answer at B2, creating two eyes at A1 and B1 for the larger group and escaping. A4 likewise allows Black an immediate reply at B2 that gains an extra liberty and breathing room toward A3. Distant moves such as D3 or C5 leave the three liberties untouched and let Black run or reinforce. The apparent atari claims for A4/B1 overlook that they do not force the full sequential capture against every possible Black continuation; only B2 does. This aligns with the liberty-count analysis already accepted by multiple colleagues.

